1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This short opening is outside the selected time range. The practical lesson starts with the next caption cue.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
So today we compare water for coffee and then practice adding milk. Keep the same beans and recipe ready.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Um, our water comparison follows the promise from the grinder lesson. We will use two plain drinking supplies.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Label the first water cup with a simple letter. Label the second water cup differently before pouring either one.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Keep the water amount equal and the grind size unchanged. We want to compare the liquid without changing the recipe.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
You know, water can taste different before brewing begins. Taste each sample plain and write a short description.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Use the first water sample for one coffee and the second for another. Keep the cup weights equal.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Our first water sample tastes soft to us. The second water sample has a slightly dry finish in this exercise.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
And so these notes describe our samples only. They do not establish which water supply another person should choose.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Hold the grind size steady through both cups. Record water temperature beside the final brew time in the notebook.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Can the plain water tasting predict the finished coffee? Our two cups suggest a difference but cannot settle that question.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The second coffee has a drier finish in this example. Repeat the water comparison tomorrow before drawing a stronger conclusion.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Now add a measured splash of milk to each cup. Record the amount so the comparison remains easy to repeat.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Like, the milk changes the texture immediately. Describe the feel with simple words before deciding which cup you prefer.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Warm milk gently in a clean jug and stir before pouring. We are observing texture rather than practicing decorative patterns.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Keep one small cup without milk as a reference. Tasting it again helps us notice what the addition changed.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Our notebook needs a separate line for the milk amount. Write down whether the drink feels lighter or thicker.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
A final water rinse clears the jug after use. Clean tools make it easier to repeat the same small routine.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
We will keep foam patterns for a future video. Today the comparison is about taste and texture in plain cups.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Leave the grind size recorded at the top of the page. That setting still connects this exercise to our earlier cups.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Water remains part of the recipe even when milk is added. Keep those water notes alongside the tasting descriptions.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Finish by reading all three notebook pages together. Choose one observation to repeat before changing another part of the recipe.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The next practice session should begin from a recorded result. Small repeatable comparisons help us understand our own preferences.
